1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,840
Centuries ago there lived—“A king!” my little readers will say immediately.

2
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:11,970
No, children, you are mistaken. Once upon a time there was a piece of wood.

3
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:16,680
It was not an expensive piece of wood. Far from it.

4
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:27,060
Just a common block of firewood, one of those thick, solid logs that are put on the fire in the winter, to make cold rooms cozy and warm.

5
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:37,790
I do not know how this really happened, yet the fact remains that one fine day this piece of wood found itself in the shop of an old carpenter.

6
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:50,340
His name was Mastro Antonio, but everybody called him Mastro Cherry, for the tip of his nose was so round and red and shiny that it looked like a ripe cherry.

7
00:00:50,520 --> 00:01:00,410
As soon as he saw that piece of wood, Mastro Cherry was filled with joy. Rubbing his hands together happily, he mumbled half to himself.

8
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:06,270
“This has come in the nick of time. I shall use it to make the leg of a table.”

9
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:11,640
He grasped the hatchet quickly to peel off the bark and shape the wood.

10
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:22,750
But as he was about to give it the first blow, he stood still with arm uplifted, for he had heard a wee little voice say in a beseeching tone.

11
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:26,540
“Please be careful. Do not hit me so hard.”

12
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:35,090
With a look of surprise shone on Mastro Cherry’s face, his funny face became funnier still.

13
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:43,500
He turned frightened eyes about the room to find out where that wee little voice had come from, and he saw no one.

14
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:52,880
He looked under the bench—no one. He peeped inside the closet—no one. He searched among the shavings—no one.

15
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,680
He opened the door to look up and down the street—and still no one.

16
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:10,220
“Oh, I see,” he said, laughing and scratching his wig. “It can easily be seen that I only thought I heard the tiny voice say the words. Well, well—to work once more.”

17
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:19,230
He struck a most solemn blow upon the piece of wood. “Oh, oh, you hurt!” cried the same far-away little voice.

18
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:28,340
Mastro Cherry grew dumb. His eyes popped out of his head, his mouth wide open, and his tongue hung down on his chin.

19
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:35,550
As soon as he regained the use of his senses, he said, trembling and stuttering from fright.

20
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:39,900
“Where did that voice come from, when there is no one around?

21
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,350
Might it be that this piece of wood has learned to weep and cry like a child? I can hardly believe it.

22
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:58,070
Here it is—a piece of common firewood, good only to burn in the stove, the same as any other. Yet—might someone be hidden in it?

23
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:02,090
If so, the worse for him. I’ll fix him!”

24
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:08,450
With these words, he grabbed the log with both hands and started to knock it about unmercifully.

25
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:14,510
He threw it to the floor, against the walls of the room, and even up to the ceiling.

26
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:24,160
He listened for the tiny voice to moan and cry. He waited two minutes—nothing; five minutes—nothing; ten minutes—nothing.

27
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:38,780
“Oh, I see,” he said, trying bravely to laugh and ruffling up his wig with his hand. “It can easily be seen I only imagined I heard the tiny voice. Well, well—to work once more.”

28
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:45,470
The poor fellow was scared half to death, so he tried to sing a gay song in order to gain courage.

29
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:58,340
He set aside the hatchet and picked up the plane to make the wood smooth and even, but as he drew it to and fro, he heard the same tiny voice. This time it giggled as it spoke.

30
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:03,990
“Ha, ha, stop it, stop it! Ha, ha, you tickle my stomach.”

31
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:08,430
This time poor Mastro Cherry fell as if shot.

32
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:12,440
When he opened his eyes, he found himself sitting on the floor.

33
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:14,450
His face had changed.

34
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:22,430
Fright had turned even the tip of his nose from red to deepest purple.
